class Student {
int id;
String name;
int marks;
```

void eat() {

```java
System.out.println(name + " student is eating");
}
```

void sleep() {

```java
System.out.println(name + " student is sleeping");
}
```

void study() {

```java
System.out.println(name + " student is studying");
}
}
class StudentApp {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Student s1 = new Student();
Student s2 = new Student();
s1.id = 1;
s1.name = "Sahana";
s1.marks = 55;
s2.id = 2;
s2.name = "Omkar";
s2.marks = 99;
System.out.println(s1.id + " " + s1.name + " " + s1.marks);
s1.eat();
s1.sleep();
s1.study();
System.out.println(s2.id + " " + s2.name + " " + s2.marks);
s2.eat();
s2.sleep();
s2.study();
}
}
```

Output 1 Sahana 55 Sahana student is eating Sahana student is sleeping Sahana student is studying 2 Omkar 99 Omkar student is eating Omkar student is sleeping Omkar student is studying Explanation Student defines what a student has and what a student does. id, name, and marks are the properties. eat(), sleep(), and study() are the behaviours. s1 stores the address of the first student object. s2 stores the address of the second student object. Values are assigned using the corresponding reference. Behaviours are invoked using the corresponding reference.

